1. Cousin brother / Cousin sister

❌ He is my cousin brother.
✅ He is my cousin.

In English, "cousin" already specifies the relationship — there's no male/female distinction needed. Adding "brother" or "sister" is redundant (this is an Indian-English influence, not standard English).
But can be used as

I went to temple along with my cousin sister(As here there is no pronoun that specifies the gender of the cousin)

2. Coward person

❌ He is a coward person.
✅ He is a coward.

"Coward" is already a noun by itself — you don't need "person" after it.
3. Passing marks / Pass marks

❌ What is the passing marks in Mathematics?
✅ What is the pass mark in Mathematics?

Correct form: "pass mark(s)" — "passing marks" is the commonly-used but incorrect version.

4. Link road / Linking road

❌ Which is the linking road to the city?
✅ Which is the link road to the city?

"Link road" is the correct compound noun; "linking" is wrong here.
5. Sign / Signature

❌ Put your sign on the documents.
✅ Put your signature on the documents.

"Sign" is a verb ("to sign"); the noun for what you write is "signature." "Put your sign" is a very common Indian-English error.
6. Photo / Photograph

❌ The documents should have your photo attached.
✅ Both "photo" and "photograph" are technically acceptable, but "photograph" is the more formal/correct term in exams — "photo" is informal/colloquial.

7. Pickpocket / Pickpocketer

❌ He is a pickpocketer.
✅ He is a pickpocket.

"Pickpocket" itself is already the noun for the person who does it — there's no "-er" form.


