13. ❌ I already have the equipments required for the work.

✅ I already have the equipment required for the work.

"Equipment" is an uncountable noun — like "furniture," "information," "advice" — it never takes an "s," no matter how many individual items are meant.

❌ equipments, furnitures, informations, advices
✅ equipment, furniture, information, advice

14. ❌ The train which met with an accident was carrying some army personnels.

✅ The train which met with an accident was carrying some army personnel.

"Personnel" is already a collective/plural-in-meaning noun (like "police" or "staff") — it refers to a group of people and never takes an "s."

❌ personnels, staffs, polices (as in "the polices")
✅ personnel, staff, police


You're right — I missed that one. Let's fix it:

❌ The authority didn't pay heed to his matter.
✅ The authorities didn't pay heed to his matter.

Why "authorities" (plural) is needed here:

When referring to people in power/officials (government bodies, administrators, police, etc.) who are responsible for taking action, English uses "authorities" (plural) — not "authority" (singular).

"Authority" (singular) = the power/right to do something, or a single governing body referred to abstractly.

He has the authority to sign this document.
The local authority (singular, referring to one specific municipal body) approved the plan.

"Authorities" (plural) = the people/officials collectively responsible for enforcing rules or taking action — this is the sense used when talking about "they didn't respond," "they ignored," etc.
